module gunbc.design.instrument_bob


data instrument_bob_note: String = "THE FISHING-BOBBER RESPONSE: the inner display takes a knock, rides up and down, and settles. Three properties make it a modelled behaviour rather than an animation, and each is checkable rather than asserted. It is BOUNDED — the envelope can never carry the plate past the register's existing attraction travel, so instrument character still cannot move a subject as far as a real response does. It SETTLES — the amplitude decays geometrically, so the return to rest carries Strict descent evidence and a derived finite settle time instead of a hope. And it is INERT — the bob is a renderer-local displacement that changes no roadmap fact, no acceptance, no focus and no operation state, which is the same law every other instrument channel already obeys.

It is a CAUSED response, which is what makes it admissible at all: this register has no ambient-motion constructor by design, and the plate is still until something knocks it. A knock is a PhysicalImpulse, and the impulse does not say where it came from — a hand on a chassis, a phone's accelerometer, a Windows sensor, or the deterministic synthetic tap all arrive here identically, so the physics is written once and never learns which platform is underneath it."

data envelope_model_note: String = "WHY AN ENVELOPE RATHER THAN AN INTEGRATED SPRING. A discrete spring integration in integer arithmetic has to be argued into stability — a step size and a stiffness can be chosen that make it grow rather than decay, and the argument that they were not is exactly the kind of claim that holds until someone retunes a constant. A geometric envelope makes the decay STRUCTURAL: the amplitude at any time is the initial amplitude scaled by a factor strictly below one raised to the number of elapsed half-lives, so it cannot grow, and no choice of constants makes it grow. The oscillation rides inside that envelope and can never exceed it. So boundedness and settling are properties of the shape rather than of the tuning, which is the difference between a construction and a check (DESIGN §5)."

data bob_max_travel_px: Int = 9

data bob_bound_note: String = "Nine pixels, and the number is derived rather than picked: the register's attraction travel is the ceiling any instrument character must stay under, and the bob is the largest single channel, so it takes a majority of that budget while leaving headroom for the resting character that coexists with it. The relationship is asserted below rather than trusted — a later edit that raised this past the register's bound reds."

data bob_idle_character_px: Int = 2

data idle_character_note: String = "The resting character is a couple of pixels: enough that the plate reads as a physical object with a little life rather than a rectangle, small enough that nobody watching it would call it motion. It is the floor the bob returns TO, not a separate animation, and the caused response is several times larger precisely so that a knock is unmistakably a knock."

data bob_half_life_ms: std.types.Milliseconds = 180

data bob_visibility_floor_milli_px: Int = 250

data settle_definition_note: String = "Settled means the envelope has fallen below a quarter of a pixel, which is under the smallest displacement any renderer can show. Defining rest as a visibility floor rather than as exact zero is what makes the settle time FINITE and derivable: a geometric decay never reaches zero, so a model that waited for zero would describe a bob that never stops, and the honest bound is the point past which continuing to move is not observable."

type BobPhase
  = BobAtRest
  | BobRinging
  | BobSettled

fn bob_phase_key(p: BobPhase) -> String {
  match p {
    BobAtRest => "at-rest"
    BobRinging => "ringing"
    BobSettled => "settled"
  }
}

data amplitude_is_derived_not_accumulated_note: String = "THE STATE CARRIES THE INITIAL AMPLITUDE AND A CLOCK, NEVER A RUNNING AMPLITUDE, AND THAT IS THE REPAIR FOR A REAL DEFECT. The previous shape stored the current amplitude and decayed it by each STEP's duration, so a sixteen-millisecond frame against a one-hundred-and-eighty-millisecond half-life computed zero half-lives and multiplied by one — the envelope never fell. The bob rang at full reach until the frame budget ran out and the renderer snapped it to zero, which is exactly the fabricated plausible output DESIGN forbids: a model that does not settle, rendered as though it had.

Deriving the amplitude from CUMULATIVE elapsed time makes the step size irrelevant. The envelope is a function of time rather than an accumulator, so no choice of frame interval can lose decay, and a caller cannot advance in a way that disagrees with a caller who advanced differently to the same instant."

type BobState {
  initial_milli_px: Int
  elapsed_ms: std.types.Milliseconds
  phase: BobPhase
}

data bob_at_rest: BobState = BobState {
  initial_milli_px: 0,
  elapsed_ms: 0,
  phase: BobAtRest,
}

data impulse_scaling_note: String = "HOW HARD A KNOCK BECOMES HOW FAR THE PLATE MOVES, and the clamp is the load-bearing half. A smack is not a proportional input: a hand can deliver an order of magnitude more acceleration than a fingertip, and a response that scaled linearly would either be invisible for a tap or would throw the plate off the screen for a real knock. So magnitude maps into the envelope and is then CLAMPED at the bound, which means the strongest possible impulse and an absurd one produce the same maximum travel. The clamp is applied at construction, so an over-bound state has no way to exist rather than being caught later."

data impulse_reference_magnitude_milli: Int = 4000

fn clamp_amplitude(milli: Int) -> Int {
  if milli > bob_max_travel_px * 1000 {
    bob_max_travel_px * 1000
  } else if milli < 0 {
    0
  } else {
    milli
  }
}

fn amplitude_for_impulse(magnitude_milli_m_per_s2: Int) -> Int {
  clamp_amplitude(milli: (magnitude_milli_m_per_s2 * bob_max_travel_px * 1000) / impulse_reference_magnitude_milli)
}

data motion_preference_note: String = "Reduced motion removes the movement and keeps the receipt. That distinction is the whole content of the arm: a viewer who has asked for less motion still gets a real, typed, visible statement that an impulse arrived, what its magnitude was, and which binding produced it — they simply do not get a plate that moves. Suppressing the receipt along with the displacement would turn a preference about animation into a loss of information, and an impulse that silently did nothing would be indistinguishable from a sensor that never fired."

type MotionPreference
  = MotionFull
  | MotionReduced

fn motion_preference_key(m: MotionPreference) -> String {
  match m {
    MotionFull => "full"
    MotionReduced => "reduced"
  }
}

fn apply_impulse(i: gunbc.capability_binding.PhysicalImpulse, m: MotionPreference) -> BobState {
  match m {
    MotionReduced => bob_at_rest
    MotionFull => BobState {
      initial_milli_px: amplitude_for_impulse(magnitude_milli_m_per_s2: i.magnitude_milli_m_per_s2),
      elapsed_ms: 0,
      phase: BobRinging,
    }
  }
}

data decay_note: String = "The envelope halves every half-life. Integer arithmetic does this by repeated halving rather than by a power function, which is both exact and obviously monotone: each step divides by two, so the sequence cannot rise, and it reaches the visibility floor in a number of steps bounded by the initial amplitude's magnitude."

fn halve_n_times(milli: Int, n: Int) -> Int {
  if n <= 0 {
    milli
  } else {
    halve_n_times(milli: milli / 2, n: n - 1)
  }
}

data interpolation_note: String = "BETWEEN TWO HALF-LIFE BOUNDARIES THE ENVELOPE FALLS LINEARLY, because halving alone is a staircase. At a sixteen-millisecond frame and a one-hundred-and-eighty-millisecond half-life, pure halving holds the amplitude flat for eleven frames and then drops it by half in one — a step, not a decay, and a viewer would see a plate that rings at one reach and then jumps. Interpolating inside the segment costs one multiply and makes every frame carry a different, smaller envelope than the frame before it.

It stays MONOTONE by construction rather than by tuning: the subtrahend is a non-decreasing function of the offset into the segment, and at the segment's end it has removed exactly half, which is the value the next boundary independently produces. So the interpolated curve and the halving lattice agree at every boundary and the sequence cannot rise between them."

fn amplitude_at(initial_milli: Int, elapsed_ms: std.types.Milliseconds) -> Int {
  let whole = halve_n_times(milli: initial_milli, n: elapsed_ms / bob_half_life_ms)
  let into_segment = elapsed_ms % bob_half_life_ms
  whole - ((whole * into_segment) / (bob_half_life_ms * 2))
}

fn bob_amplitude_milli(s: BobState) -> Int {
  match s.phase {
    BobAtRest => 0
    BobSettled => 0
    BobRinging => amplitude_at(initial_milli: s.initial_milli_px, elapsed_ms: s.elapsed_ms)
  }
}

fn advance(s: BobState, dt_ms: std.types.Milliseconds) -> BobState {
  let next_elapsed = s.elapsed_ms + dt_ms
  let next_amp = amplitude_at(initial_milli: s.initial_milli_px, elapsed_ms: next_elapsed)
  if next_amp < bob_visibility_floor_milli_px {
    BobState { initial_milli_px: s.initial_milli_px, elapsed_ms: next_elapsed, phase: BobSettled }
  } else {
    BobState { initial_milli_px: s.initial_milli_px, elapsed_ms: next_elapsed, phase: BobRinging }
  }
}

data descent_note: String = "The settle claim, carried as evidence rather than as prose — and MEASURED rather than inferred from the step size. The previous version read the evidence off dt alone: any advance shorter than a half-life was declared NonIncreasing without ever comparing two amplitudes. That is a claim about the envelope derived from something other than the envelope, and it stayed green through the whole period in which the envelope did not fall at all.

Comparing the two amplitudes makes the evidence a fact about what happened. Strict when the envelope actually decreased, NonIncreasing when integer arithmetic left it equal near the floor, and DescentUnknown — which is the fail-closed bottom of the lattice — if it ever GREW. A model that started growing would surface here as lost evidence rather than as a bob that looked wrong."

fn advance_descent_evidence(s: BobState, dt_ms: std.types.Milliseconds) -> std.termination.DescentEvidence {
  match s.phase {
    BobAtRest => Strict
    BobSettled => Strict
    BobRinging => {
      let before = bob_amplitude_milli(s: s)
      let after = bob_amplitude_milli(s: advance(s: s, dt_ms: dt_ms))
      if after < before {
        Strict
      } else if after == before {
        NonIncreasing
      } else {
        DescentUnknown
      }
    }
  }
}

data settle_bound_note: String = "The derived finite settle time: the number of half-lives it takes the largest admissible envelope to fall under the visibility floor, times the half-life. It is a DERIVED bound rather than a declared timeout, so retuning the bound, the floor or the half-life moves it automatically and no second number goes stale."

fn half_lives_to_settle(initial_milli: Int) -> Int {
  if initial_milli < bob_visibility_floor_milli_px {
    0
  } else {
    1 + half_lives_to_settle(initial_milli: initial_milli / 2)
  }
}

fn settle_time_ms(initial_milli: Int) -> std.types.Milliseconds {
  half_lives_to_settle(initial_milli: initial_milli) * bob_half_life_ms
}

fn max_settle_time_ms() -> std.types.Milliseconds {
  settle_time_ms(initial_milli: bob_max_travel_px * 1000)
}

data boundedness_note: String = "The bound stated as a predicate over every reachable state rather than as a property of the constructor alone, so it also covers states produced by advancing. It is the claim the register's own displacement law needs: instrument character never travels as far as a real response."

fn bob_is_within_register_bound(s: BobState) -> Bool {
  bob_amplitude_milli(s: s) <= bob_max_travel_px * 1000
    && s.initial_milli_px <= bob_max_travel_px * 1000
    && bob_max_travel_px < attraction_travel_max_px
    && bob_max_travel_px <= instrument_displacement_max_px
}

data inertness_note: String = "The bob rides the existing impulse-glitch channel, so it inherits that channel's law without restating it: no instrument channel may carry a domain fact. The predicate is re-asserted here as a claim rather than re-implemented, which is the difference between citing an authority and forking one."

fn bob_carries_no_domain_fact() -> Bool {
  !channel_may_carry_domain_fact(c: ChannelImpulseGlitch)
}

data displacement_projection_note: String = "The renderer receives whole pixels; the model keeps milli-pixels so the decay does not quantise to zero prematurely. Rounding happens once, at the boundary, which is where the target's own units demand it."

fn bob_displacement_px(s: BobState) -> Int {
  bob_amplitude_milli(s: s) / 1000
}

data oscillation_note: String = "A BOBBER CROSSES THE WATERLINE, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES IT A BOB RATHER THAN A SAG — AND IT PASSES THROUGH THE WATERLINE RATHER THAN TELEPORTING ACROSS IT. The envelope above says how far the plate may travel at a given moment; this says where between the two extremes it currently is. The previous version answered only -1 or +1, which is a square wave: every frame sat at an extreme and the crossing happened between two frames, so the plate jumped from full-down to full-up and never occupied any position in between. It satisfied a claim that the sequence contains values on both sides of rest while showing a motion nobody would call a bob.

A triangle answers the same question with the intermediate positions present. It starts AT REST and travels down, because a knock pushes the plate away from the hand rather than toward it, reaches full reach a quarter-period in, returns through rest, rises to the opposite extreme, and comes back — the shape a float actually makes, sampled at whatever interval the renderer asks for. Period and half-life stay independent constants on purpose: the half-life decides how long the ringing lasts, the period how quickly it oscillates within that."

data bob_half_period_ms: std.types.Milliseconds = 90

data oscillation_unit_milli: Int = 1000

fn bob_quarter_period_ms() -> std.types.Milliseconds {
  bob_half_period_ms / 2
}

data quarter_leg_note: String = "Four legs make one full period, and each is a straight line between a zero and an extreme. Leg 0 falls from rest to full down, leg 1 returns to rest, leg 2 rises to full up, leg 3 returns. Naming the legs rather than writing one closed form keeps each endpoint checkable by inspection, and the endpoints are what the crossing claim depends on."

fn oscillation_leg_milli(leg: Int, into_leg: std.types.Milliseconds) -> Int {
  let travelled = (oscillation_unit_milli * into_leg) / bob_quarter_period_ms()
  if leg == 0 {
    0 - travelled
  } else if leg == 1 {
    travelled - oscillation_unit_milli
  } else if leg == 2 {
    travelled
  } else {
    oscillation_unit_milli - travelled
  }
}

fn oscillation_milli(elapsed_ms: std.types.Milliseconds) -> Int {
  oscillation_leg_milli(
    leg: (elapsed_ms / bob_quarter_period_ms()) % 4,
    into_leg: elapsed_ms % bob_quarter_period_ms())
}

data first_excursion_is_downward_note: String = "The first leg is negative because a knock on a chassis pushes the plate DOWN before it rebounds. Starting upward would read as the screen jumping toward the hand that hit it. It begins at exactly zero, so the sequence starts at rest rather than starting already displaced."

fn signed_displacement_milli(s: BobState) -> Int {
  (bob_amplitude_milli(s: s) * oscillation_milli(elapsed_ms: s.elapsed_ms)) / oscillation_unit_milli
}

fn signed_displacement_px(s: BobState) -> Int {
  signed_displacement_milli(s: s) / 1000
}

data frame_sequence_note: String = "THE DETERMINISTIC FRAME SEQUENCE, which is what makes the bob judgeable without a browser. Given an impulse and a preference, the whole response is a finite list of frames — a time, a signed displacement, and a phase — computed with no clock, no randomness and no host. The same impulse always yields byte-identical frames, so a renderer becomes a consumer of a decided sequence rather than the place the motion is invented, and a regression in the physics is a diff over a list rather than something someone has to watch for.

The recursion is bounded by a frame budget derived from the settle time rather than by waiting for the settled phase, so termination is structural: the list cannot be longer than the budget even if the envelope were somehow changed to stop decaying."

type BobFrame {
  at_ms: std.types.Milliseconds
  displacement_px: Int
  phase: BobPhase
}

data frame_budget_is_a_count_not_a_duration_note: String = "A FRAME COUNT IS NOT A DURATION, AND THE SUBSTRATE CAUGHT IT TWICE. Dividing the settle time by a frame interval is arithmetic on two Milliseconds values, and the branded carrier propagates through the quotient — so a branch returning a bare zero disagreed with a branch returning a duration, and the typechecker refused rather than handing back a count wearing a time unit.

The first repair reached for a cast and was refused in turn, which was the more instructive answer: Milliseconds is a REFINEMENT of Int rather than a separate representation, so 'as Int' is a cast from Int to Int and means nothing. The brand is not something a value carries and can shed on request; it is something a POSITION requires. So the honest move is to let the brand fall away at a boundary that asks for a plain Int — frame_budget takes counts and durations as bare magnitudes and returns a count, and max_settle_time_ms flows into it because a Milliseconds is admissible wherever an Int is. No conversion happens because none is needed; what changes is which type the position demands."

fn frame_budget(settle_ms: Int, interval: Int) -> Int {
  if interval <= 0 {
    0
  } else {
    (settle_ms / interval) + 2
  }
}

fn max_frame_count(interval_ms: std.types.Milliseconds) -> Int {
  frame_budget(settle_ms: max_settle_time_ms(), interval: interval_ms)
}

data budget_exhaustion_is_a_refusal_note: String = "A TRUNCATED SEQUENCE IS NOT A SHORTER SUCCESS. The previous shape returned the empty list when the frame budget ran out, so a sequence that never reached rest was indistinguishable from one that did — and the renderer, having consumed the list, set the plate back to zero on its own. Between them they manufactured a settled bob out of a model that had not settled: the deficit's frequency was zero by construction and nothing could ever rank it for fixing (DESIGN §5, the absorbing fallback).

So exhaustion REFUSES, typed and located, carrying how many frames it managed and what budget it was given. The consumer cannot render a refusal as motion because a refusal is not a list, and the page has no arm that turns one into a plausible wire. That is also why the wire ships displacement only: the alternative the review offered — shipping time and phase so a consumer could check settling itself — is unnecessary once the sequence a consumer receives is, by construction, one that reached BobSettled."

type BobSequenceRefusal
  = BudgetExhaustedBeforeSettling
  | NonPositiveFrameInterval

fn bob_sequence_refusal_key(r: BobSequenceRefusal) -> String {
  match r {
    BudgetExhaustedBeforeSettling => "budget-exhausted-before-settling"
    NonPositiveFrameInterval => "non-positive-frame-interval"
  }
}

data budget_allowed_not_remaining_note: String = "THE FIELD SAYS WHAT IT MEANS. An earlier revision reported the REMAINING budget at the moment of exhaustion — which is always zero — under a name a reader takes for the budget the sequence was given, and the control pinned that zero, making the inaccurate receipt load-bearing. A number rendered under a name it does not mean is worse than an absent number, because it reads as information. The allowed budget is threaded down so the refusal can name the bound it actually failed against."

type BobSequence
  = BobSequenceSettled { frames: List<BobFrame> }
  | BobSequenceRefused { cause: BobSequenceRefusal, frames_emitted: Int, budget_allowed: Int }

fn bob_frame_of(s: BobState) -> BobFrame {
  BobFrame {
    at_ms: s.elapsed_ms,
    displacement_px: signed_displacement_px(s: s),
    phase: s.phase,
  }
}

fn bob_frames_from(s: BobState, interval_ms: std.types.Milliseconds, budget: Int, budget_allowed: Int) -> BobSequence {
  if budget <= 0 {
    BobSequenceRefused {
      cause: BudgetExhaustedBeforeSettling,
      frames_emitted: 0,
      budget_allowed: budget_allowed,
    }
  } else {
    let next = advance(s: s, dt_ms: interval_ms)
    let frame = bob_frame_of(s: next)
    match next.phase {
      BobSettled => BobSequenceSettled { frames: [frame] }
      BobAtRest => BobSequenceSettled { frames: [frame] }
      BobRinging =>
        match bob_frames_from(s: next, interval_ms: interval_ms, budget: budget - 1, budget_allowed: budget_allowed) {
          BobSequenceSettled { frames: rest } => BobSequenceSettled { frames: concat([frame], rest) }
          BobSequenceRefused { cause: c, frames_emitted: n, budget_allowed: a } =>
            BobSequenceRefused { cause: c, frames_emitted: n + 1, budget_allowed: a }
        }
    }
  }
}

fn bob_sequence(i: gunbc.capability_binding.PhysicalImpulse, m: MotionPreference, interval_ms: std.types.Milliseconds) -> BobSequence {
  if interval_ms <= 0 {
    BobSequenceRefused { cause: NonPositiveFrameInterval, frames_emitted: 0, budget_allowed: 0 }
  } else {
    let start = apply_impulse(i: i, m: m)
    match start.phase {
      BobAtRest => BobSequenceSettled { frames: [bob_frame_of(s: start)] }
      BobSettled => BobSequenceSettled { frames: [bob_frame_of(s: start)] }
      BobRinging =>
        match bob_frames_from(
          s: start,
          interval_ms: interval_ms,
          budget: max_frame_count(interval_ms: interval_ms),
          budget_allowed: max_frame_count(interval_ms: interval_ms),
        ) {
          BobSequenceSettled { frames: fs } =>
            BobSequenceSettled { frames: concat([bob_frame_of(s: start)], fs) }
          BobSequenceRefused { cause: c, frames_emitted: n, budget_allowed: a } =>
            BobSequenceRefused { cause: c, frames_emitted: n, budget_allowed: a }
        }
    }
  }
}

data starts_at_rest_note: String = "The start state is emitted as its own frame, so the sequence literally begins at rest at time zero rather than beginning already displaced one interval in. It costs one frame and it is what makes 'starts at rest, travels downward' a property of the shipped wire rather than of the reader's charity."

data no_frames_or_empty_helper_note: String = "THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO HELPER THAT TURNS A REFUSAL INTO AN EMPTY LIST. One existed, and it reintroduced the absorbing fallback at a second seam one layer above the one the typed sequence had just closed: a refused sequence became [], the wire became the empty string, and a consumer asserting that the page contains that wire passed vacuously because every string contains the empty string. Every consumer matches the coproduct, which costs two lines and cannot be satisfied by a refusal."

data frame_bound_note: String = "Every frame in a sequence stays inside the envelope in BOTH directions, which is the bound restated over the signed value: a plate that travelled nine pixels down would violate the register just as surely as one that travelled nine pixels up."

fn frame_is_within_bound(f: BobFrame) -> Bool {
  f.displacement_px <= bob_max_travel_px && f.displacement_px >= (0 - bob_max_travel_px)
}

fn frames_are_within_bound(fs: List<BobFrame>) -> Bool {
  all(fs, f => frame_is_within_bound(f: f))
}

data terminates_once_note: String = "Exactly one frame in a sequence is settled, and counting is a stronger claim than checking the last one: it says the sequence reaches rest AND stops there, so a recursion that kept emitting frames past settling would fail even though its final frame looked correct."

fn settled_frame_count(fs: List<BobFrame>) -> Int {
  fold(fs, init: 0, f: (acc, x) => if bob_phase_key(p: x.phase) == "settled" { acc + 1 } else { acc })
}

fn frames_terminate_exactly_once(fs: List<BobFrame>) -> Bool {
  settled_frame_count(fs: fs) == 1
}

fn frames_cross_rest(fs: List<BobFrame>) -> Bool {
  fold(fs, init: false, f: (acc, x) => acc || x.displacement_px < 0)
    && fold(fs, init: false, f: (acc, x) => acc || x.displacement_px > 0)
}

data intermediate_positions_note: String = "A square wave contains values on both sides of rest and is still not a bob. This is the claim that separates them: the sequence must contain a displacement that is neither rest nor either extreme — a position the plate actually occupies on its way — which a two-valued waveform cannot satisfy no matter how long it runs."

fn frames_visit_an_intermediate_position(fs: List<BobFrame>) -> Bool {
  fold(fs, init: false, f: (acc, x) =>
    acc || (x.displacement_px != 0
      && x.displacement_px != bob_max_travel_px
      && x.displacement_px != (0 - bob_max_travel_px)))
}

data envelope_falls_note: String = "The reach actually diminishes over the sequence: the largest displacement in the first third is strictly greater than the largest in the last third. It is stated over the SHIPPED frames rather than over the envelope function, because the defect this replaces was one where the envelope function was fine in isolation and the sequence it produced never fell."

fn frames_max_abs_between(fs: List<BobFrame>, from_ms: Int, to_ms: Int) -> Int {
  fold(fs, init: 0, f: (acc, x) => {
    let mag = if x.displacement_px < 0 { 0 - x.displacement_px } else { x.displacement_px }
    if x.at_ms >= from_ms && x.at_ms < to_ms && mag > acc { mag } else { acc }
  })
}

fn frames_last_displacement(fs: List<BobFrame>) -> Int {
  fold(fs, init: 0, f: (acc, x) => x.displacement_px)
}

